{"name":"Caen Stone","hex":"#ecd0b1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Caen stone","blocks":[{"text":"Caen stone is a light creamy-yellow Jurassic limestone quarried in north-western France near the city of Caen. The limestone is a fine grained oolitic limestone formed in shallow water lagoons in the Bathonian Age about 167 million years ago. The stone is homogeneous, and therefore suitable for carving."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Caensaintpierre.jpg/330px-Caensaintpierre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caen_stone"},{"title":"Caen","blocks":[{"text":"Caen is a city and commune 15 km (9.3 mi) inland from the northwestern coast of France. It is the prefecture of the department of Calvados. The city proper has 105,512 inhabitants, while its functional urban area has 470,000, making Caen the second largest urban area in Normandy and the 19th largest in France. It is also the third largest commune in all of Normandy after Le Havre and Rouen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/France_Caen_Trinite_c.JPG/330px-France_Caen_Trinite_c.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caen"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Mairie_de_Caen_7.JPG/500px-Mairie_de_Caen_7.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Mairie_de_Caen_7.JPG/1280px-Mairie_de_Caen_7.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Mairie_de_Caen_7.JPG/500px-Mairie_de_Caen_7.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMairie_de_Caen_7.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Caen%2C_%C3%89glise_Saint-Pierre_01.JPG/500px-Caen%2C_%C3%89glise_Saint-Pierre_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Caen%2C_%C3%89glise_Saint-Pierre_01.JPG/500px-Caen%2C_%C3%89glise_Saint-Pierre_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACaen%2C_%C3%89glise_Saint-Pierre_01.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Caen_Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Caen_S%C3%BCdseite_08.jpg/500px-Caen_Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Ca